        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "@echasnovski, thanks for a quick response! Right, as the scope of other key maps is different, perhaps it needs further explanation. I will try to give examples of behavior and then, try to state it in general. The examples will be in the following form. I will present the state of buffer before pressing the keys, and after. May `|` denote cursor in insert mode.\r\n\r\n# `<C-w>`\r\nBefore:\r\n```\r\nfunction(|)\r\n```\r\nAfter:\r\n```\r\nfunction|\r\n```\r\nThe similar behavior occurs in plain NeoVim, with regard to the opening bracket. In general, when the cursor is after the opening brackets, the result of pressing `<C-w>` should be the same as that of `<BS>`. There is one case in which I have doubts. That is when the opening bracket isn't really a bracket. For example, the letter `a`. I would expect the following with `f` as an opening and closing bracket.\r\nBefore:\r\n```\r\nsnif|fing\r\n```\r\nAfter:\r\n```\r\n|fing\r\n```\r\nSo in this case the behavior would be different, then that of `<BS>`.\r\n\r\nI would like to mention, that both of these behaviors are consistent with NeoVim. I believe NeoVim treats such special characters as “(” as words, despite the spacing.\r\n\r\n# `<C-u>`\r\nBefore:\r\n```\r\nThis is an example (|) sentence.\r\n```\r\nAfter:\r\n```\r\n| sentence.\r\n```\r\nThis seems to be more straightforward, then `<C-w>`. Simply delete all the characters before the cursor, and the closing bracket.\r\n\r\n# `<C-h>`\r\nWith regard to the mapping of `<C-h>` to `<BS>`, I tried to do this a long time ago with some other plugin, and if I remember correctly, it resulted in unwanted side effect. I will try to dig it out, perhaps I mentioned the reason in some discussion.",
