        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks, I understand it better now.\r\n\r\nThis is an interesting idea. I'll rephrase it in terms that are more aligned with 'mini.pairs'. For both `<C-w>` and `<C-u>` act like `<BS>` is acting now:\r\n- If cursor is inside registered pair (like `aa (|) bb`), add additional `<Del>`. So for `<C-w>`, pressing it would be equivalent to typing `<C-w><Del>` without 'mini.pairs'. Same for `<C-u>`.\r\n- Otherwise, act like plain `<C-w>` or `<C-u>`.\r\n\r\nIf this is what you would expect, then I think it should be achievable relatively easy: `MiniPairs.bs()` can take a `key` variable used as base key (`<BS>` by default). Then it is a matter for users to make these mappings:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nvim.keymap.set('i', '<C-h>', 'v:lua.MiniPairs.bs()', {expr = true, replace_keycodes = false})\r\nvim.keymap.set('i', '<C-w>', 'v:lua.MiniPairs.bs(\"\\23\")', { expr = true, replace_keycodes = false })\r\nvim.keymap.set('i', '<C-u>', 'v:lua.MiniPairs.bs(\"\\21\")', { expr = true, replace_keycodes = false })\r\n```\r\n\r\nDoes this work for you?\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nI think literally mapping `<C-h>` to `<BS>` in Insert mode should be fine. Be sure to use `{ remap = true }`. If you don't like this approach, you can recreate mapping for `<BS>` itself as I've written before.",
