        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I believe the idea as you put it is correct. I don't think it acknowledges for the case where the bracket is a letter that can be inside a word, see “sniffing” above, but perhaps it is an edge case, and it would be premature to try to solve for it, if it doesn't raise in practice.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, I believe the solution as you described it would work, but the code snippet doesn't. I put it into my config, and then while in the state `(test|)`, pressing `<C-w>` results in `(tes|)`, that is it works like `<BS>` where it should work as before.\r\n\r\nAlso, with regard to mapping `<C-h>` to `<BS>`, I dug out the reason it could be crippling. It prevents you from mapping `<BS>` to something else than deleting the character. Actually, in NeoVim I don't use backspace at all, and I could map it to something else, as `<C-h>` is sufficient. Though, for now, I simply don't use it for anything, so it would be a solution. Despite, remapping this key doesn't seem unlikely to happen. `<BS>` is a truly valid key for mapping, as it is unused, but as I said, not the big deal at a moment.",
