        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Furthermore, I believe the solution as you described it would work, but the code snippet doesn't. I put it into my config, and then while in the state `(test|)`, pressing `<C-w>` results in `(tes|)`, that is it works like `<BS>` where it should work as before.\r\n\r\nYes, it needs small changes to `MiniPairs.bs()` to take key as argument and use it. I plan to add this tomorrow. If you want to pre-test it, update [these two lines](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/0c2210fe2e9e08e4dbf33af6984e48a6cc3e0b57/lua/mini/pairs.lua#L328-L329) in your local installed version of 'mini.nvim'/'mini.pairs' to be:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nMiniPairs.bs = function(key)\r\n  local res = key or H.keys.bs\r\n```\r\n\r\n> Also, with regard to mapping `<C-h>` to `<BS>`, I dug out the reason it could be crippling. It prevents you from mapping `<BS>` to something else than deleting the character. Actually, in NeoVim I don't use backspace at all, and I could map it to something else, as `<C-h>` is sufficient. Though, for now, I simply don't use it for anything, so it would be a solution. Despite, remapping this key doesn't seem unlikely to happen. `<BS>` is a truly valid key for mapping, as it is unused, but as I said, not the big deal at a moment.\r\n\r\nAgain, you can map `<C-h>` to use `v:lua.MiniPairs.bs()` as in snippet above. It should work now even without previously mentioned code change.",
